---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { courseCards } from '../lib/catalog';
import { feedbackUrl } from '../lib/feedback';
import AnswerSpace from '../components/AnswerSpace.astro';
import MadeCheckedFixed from '../components/MadeCheckedFixed.astro';
import OneIdeaLadder from '../components/OneIdeaLadder.astro';
import { MILESTONES } from '../data/updates';
import UPDATE_PRS from '../data/updates-prs.json';

/* Counted from the collection so the numbers below cannot go stale. They are shown
   as numerals: the old spelled-out version ran out of words above forty. */
const { cards, lessonCount } = await courseCards();
const courseCount = cards.length;
const bioCount = (await getCollection('lessons', ({ data }) => !data.draft && data.course === 'biochemistry')).length;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const latest = [...MILESTONES].sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : -1)).slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout title="About — Biochemistrypedia" description="What this site is, how it is made with AI, how it is checked, and how to report an error.">
  <article class="wrap prose-col" style="padding-top: var(--ddp-space-12)">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>A free ladder through science, built in public.</h1>

    <p>
      Short version: this is a free site for learning science, one rung at a time. It starts with biology.
      Today it has {fmt(lessonCount)} lessons in {fmt(courseCount)} courses. They run from hands-on activities a parent
      does with a five-year-old to courses that end at current research. Every lesson is free to read. You
      never need an account to learn, and there are no ads.
    </p>

    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>
      Picture everything people know as a circle. A small child stands at the center. Near the center the
      questions have answers. Moving outward, the answers thin. Past the edge there are more questions than
      answers, and that is where research happens.
    </p>
    <AnswerSpace />
    <p>
      To understand the open questions at the edge, you need every rung between the center and there. I am
      trying to build those rungs. Biology comes first. Chemistry, physics, math and engineering are meant to
      follow, and every rung will stay free.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can start on any rung. Nobody needs a background to begin. Every lesson explains its central idea
      at more than one level, and most run from a child’s version to a graduate student’s, so you can drop
      down a level when you need to and climb back up.
    </p>

    {/* Moved from the homepage, 2026-11-08: photosynthesis at six levels, its film, and the three promises. */}
    <OneIdeaLadder />

    <h2 id="who">Who makes it</h2>
    <p>
      I’m Chris Deppmann, Professor in the Departments of Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Cell Biology, and
      Neuroscience at the University of Virginia. I choose what goes in, direct the AI that makes it, and decide
      what gets fixed. Former trainees from my lab have volunteered to help. Students in my biochemistry course, BIOL 3030, made
      <a href="/fun">The Fun Stuff</a>: the trading cards, memes, songs and podcast.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in a lesson</h2>
    <p>
      A lesson is a short reading. Nearly every one has a short film. Most have a simulation you can change.
      All have practice questions that show the answer and explain why. If you learn on your own, your progress stays on your own device, unless you choose an optional learner account (13 and older) to sync it between your devices. If a teacher adds you to a class, your
      answers in that class go to your teacher; the <a href="/teach/privacy/">class privacy page</a> has the details.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it came from</h2>
    <p>
      It began in June 2026 as an experiment in my lab at the University of Virginia: {fmt(bioCount)} lessons for my
      undergraduate biochemistry course, built from twenty years of my lectures and my book,
      <em>The Molecule Hunters</em>. That first course gave the site its name. Biochemistry is now {fmt(bioCount)} of the
      {fmt(lessonCount)} lessons, and the site is growing into its own thing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="work-in-progress">A work in progress</h2>
    <p>
      I chose to put this up rough and improve it in public. You will find mistakes, and I want to hear about them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Parts of some lessons are still being checked. Those lessons carry an <strong>Under review</strong> tag at
      the top, and the text stays up while the check is done. Some subjects are not here yet: chemistry, physics,
      math, engineering, and anything for children under five. The <a href="/paths/mcat/">MCAT path</a> lists the
      parts of the exam the site does not cover yet.
    </p>
    <aside class="updates-teaser">
      <p class="updates-teaser-head">
        <strong>{fmt(UPDATE_PRS.length)} updates since June.</strong>
        <a href="/updates/">See what’s new and what’s next →</a>
      </p>
      <ul>
        {latest.map((m) => <li><time datetime={m.date}>{new Date(m.date + 'T12:00:00Z').toLocaleDateString('en-US', { month: 'short', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' })}</time> {m.title}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <h2>How AI is used</h2>
    <p>I use AI for most of the work, and here is where.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Writing.</strong> The lessons, questions and film scripts are human-directed and AI-made. I decide
        what goes in and direct the work. AI models write it from my lectures, my book and the research literature.
        We will keep improving it as more people send feedback and as the models get better. We look hardest at
        three things: clarity, accuracy, and whether each idea gets the emphasis it deserves.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Films.</strong> An AI draws the animations in code. An AI voice reads the narration. Every film has captions, and each lesson’s Sources &amp; Integrity panel names the voice.</li>
      <li><strong>Illustrations.</strong> A lesson can have up to five. An image model draws each one from a written description, and every detail in that description has to come from a sentence in the lesson. A second model, which did not write the description, then checks the image against the lesson, labels included. Images that fail are redrawn or dropped. Each one is marked “AI-generated illustration” in a tag under the figure, in its description for screen readers, and in the lesson’s Sources &amp; Integrity panel. They never show a chemical structure or data.</li>
      <li><strong>Scientist portraits</strong> are AI-generated artwork, and they are labelled that way.</li>
      <li><strong>Molecules</strong> are never drawn by AI. Every 3D structure loads live from a public database, RCSB PDB or PubChem, by its ID.</li>
      <li><strong>No AI tutor talks to learners.</strong> A teacher can turn on AI feedback for their own class with their own key. It is off by default, and the <a href="/teach/privacy/">class privacy page</a> says exactly what is sent.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How it is checked</h2>
    <MadeCheckedFixed />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-model checks.</strong> Every lesson has been checked by AI models from more than one
        company, claim by claim, against sources. Errors they find get corrected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Human review.</strong> We built these lessons fast, so a person has not yet checked every one by
        hand. We are working through them now. That is why your feedback matters: tell us what is wrong, and we
        will fix it fast.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Questions.</strong> Every practice and MCAT-style question was written for this site. The MCAT-style questions are original. None is a real AAMC question.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Corrections are public.</strong> Every fix goes into the site’s
        <a href="https://github.com/deppmann/biochempedia/commits/main">public change history</a> on GitHub.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Report an error</h2>
    <p>
      Every lesson has a <strong>Report an error</strong> button in its side panel. It opens a short form with the
      lesson already filled in. You do not have to say who you are. Tell me where on the page the problem is and
      what looks wrong. The same form takes requests: a topic you want covered, or something the site is missing.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also email me at
      <a href="mailto:deppmann@gmail.com?subject=Biochemistrypedia%20correction">deppmann@gmail.com</a>, which is the
      way to send a screenshot. I read every report. A confirmed error gets fixed, or the figure comes down.
    </p>
    <p><a class="report-btn" href={feedbackUrl()} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Report an error →</a></p>

    <h2>The small print</h2>
    <p>
      The site’s own films are served from its own storage. Videos and podcasts made by other people (YouTube and Spotify) are
      embedded from their source. Visits are counted anonymously, with no cookies. Learners never need to sign in; readers 13 and older can
      choose a learner account to sync notes and progress (see <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>). Teachers who make a class
      have a teacher account, and students in a class sign in with a nickname and a student code, with no learner account
      needed. Both are described on the <a href="/teach/privacy/">class privacy page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="src-meta">
      You may copy, share and adapt the lesson text, figures and films for teaching, with credit, under
      CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. The scientist profiles from <em>The Molecule Hunters</em> are all rights reserved, and the
      code is MIT-licensed and open. The <a href="/license/">licence page</a> says what that means in plain words.
    </p>

    <p><a href="/">← Home</a></p>
  </article>

  <style>
    .updates-teaser {
      margin: var(--ddp-space-6) 0;
      padding: var(--ddp-space-4) var(--ddp-space-5);
      border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line);
      border-left: 4px solid var(--ddp-color-primary);
      border-radius: 10px;
      background: var(--ddp-color-surface);
    }
    .updates-teaser-head { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.25rem 0.75rem; align-items: baseline; }
    .updates-teaser ul { margin: var(--ddp-space-2) 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-ink-soft); }
    .updates-teaser li { margin: 0.15rem 0; }
    .updates-teaser time { display: inline-block; min-width: 3.6em; color: var(--ddp-color-muted); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
    .eyebrow {
      font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono);
      font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs);
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ddp-color-accent);
    }
    .src-meta {
      color: var(--ddp-color-muted);
      font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm);
    }
    .report-btn {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      min-height: 44px;
      padding: 0.5em 1.2em;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: var(--ddp-color-primary);
      color: var(--ddp-color-surface);
      font-weight: 700;
      text-decoration: none;
    }
    .report-btn:hover { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ddp-color-surface); box-shadow: var(--ddp-shadow); }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
